    "result_text": "The full test suite is green: **255 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented a rule that rejects owner creation with **429 Too Many Requests** once 100 or more owners have already been created today (by `registrationDate`), following the project's OfficeFloor function-injection conventions (mirroring the existing city-capacity rule).\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`RejectOwnerDailyLimit.java`** (new function) \u2014 counts existing owners whose `registrationDate` equals the current server date; if `>= 100` (`DAILY_LIMIT`), throws a checked `OwnerDailyLimitException`. Excludes the owner itself, matching the pattern used by the other reject steps.\n\n2. **`OwnerDailyLimitException.java`** (new checked exception) \u2014 so it surfaces in the `throws` clause for escalation matching.\n\n3. **`OwnerDailyLimitExceptionHandler.java`** (new escalation handler) \u2014 builds a `ProblemDetail` and responds `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429), consistent with the other handlers.\n\n4. **`officefloor/escalation/\u2026OwnerDailyLimitException.yml`** (new) \u2014 wires the exception to its handler.\n\n5. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `rejectDailyLimit` step into the pipeline after `rejectCityCapacity` and before `assignHousehold`, under the same `transaction` governance. It runs after `defaultRegistrationDate`, so the incoming owner's registration date is populated first.\n\n6. **`OwnerRestControllerV1Tests.java`** \u2014 added `createOwnerDailyLimitReached`, which seeds 100 owners registered today (with distinct city/telephone/household fields so only the daily-limit rule applies) and asserts the next POST returns 429 with the expected problem title.\n\nThe daily-limit check is positioned after the telephone/household/city-capacity rejections in the pipeline; those still take precedence for their respective conflict cases, and the new test uses distinct values so it isolates the 429 path.",
